<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thebes, Greece</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a> was the leading city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a> in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and the dominant member of the Boeotian League. It sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> during the invasion of 480–479 BCE, but under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epaminondas">Epaminondas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pelopidas">Pelopidas</a> it defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-leuctra">Leuctra</a> in 371 BCE and briefly became the strongest power in Greece. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> destroyed the city in 335 BCE after it revolted.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thebes-greece.jpg" alt="Thebes, Greece" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chabe01 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vue_Nord_Ville_-_Th%C3%A8bes_(GR03)_-_2022-03-21_-_3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Excavations in and around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a> have revealed a Mycenaean settlement, cist graves holding weapons and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, and clay tablets in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-b">Linear B</a>, which show its importance in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cadmea">Cadmea</a>, the central citadel, shows signs of destruction towards the end of the Mycenaean era, and much of the site was abandoned. Thebes was the setting of myths about <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cadmus">Cadmus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oedipus">Oedipus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysus">Dionysus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Heracles</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a> calls it Seven-Gated Thebes to distinguish it from the city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. In 480 to 479 BCE a contingent of 400 Thebans stood at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-thermopylae">Thermopylae</a>, but the governing aristocracy then joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes I</a> and fought for him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Plataea</a>, and the victorious <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> took away the city's presidency of the Boeotian League.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a> was a firm ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a>, but it broke with Sparta afterwards. A Spartan force seized the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cadmea">Cadmea</a> in 382 BCE; three years later the garrison was expelled and a democratic constitution replaced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oligarchy">oligarchy</a>. The army trained and led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epaminondas">Epaminondas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pelopidas">Pelopidas</a>, which included the Sacred Band of Thebes, defeated the Spartans at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-leuctra">Leuctra</a> in 371 BCE. Thebes then led a large coalition into Peloponnesus and crippled Sparta, partly by freeing many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helots">helots</a>. Its predominance was short, and after Epaminondas died at Mantinea in 362 BCE it fell back to a secondary power.</p><p>Thebes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> lost the Battle of Chaeronea to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a> in 338 BCE. After an unsuccessful revolt in 335 BCE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> and his Greek allies destroyed the city, except, according to tradition, the house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pindar">Pindar</a> and the temples, divided its territory among other Boeotian cities and sold its people into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. Ancient writers treat this as excessive. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassander">Cassander</a> re-established Thebes in 315 BCE, but it never regained its prominence, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrius-i-of-macedon">Demetrius Poliorcetes</a> besieged it in 293 and 292 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Thebes came under Roman rule after the Boeotian League was dissolved in 146 BCE, and in the late 7th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-ii">Justinian II</a> made it the capital of the Theme of Hellas. By the middle of the 12th century it was the biggest producer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silks</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>, until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> plundered it in 1147 and took craftsmen to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a>. The city conquered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a> in 1205 is today the largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-greece">Central Greece</a>. It has an archaeological museum, remains of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cadmea">Cadmea</a> and the church of Luke the Evangelist, which holds the saint's tomb and relics.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Thebes, Greece” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Athens, urged on by Demosthenes, joined <b>Thebes</b> against Philip II of Macedon and was defeated at the Battle of Chaeronea in 338 BCE; Philip treated the city leniently, and it joined his League of Corinth.</blockquote><a href="/empire/athens">Athens · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The young Philip II of Macedon spent several years as a hostage at <b>Thebes</b> in the 360s BCE, at the height of Theban power, and probably learned from its generals.</blockquote><a href="/empire/boeotian-league">Boeotian League · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>…Paeonians, took Amphipolis, Pydna and Potidaea in 357–356 BCE, crushed Onomarchus at the Battle of Crocus Field in 352 BCE, destroyed Olynthos in 348 BCE and, after failed sieges of Perinthus and Byzantion in 340 BCE, defeated Athens and <b>Thebes</b> at the Battle of Chaeronea in 338 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-macedon">Kingdom of Macedon · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After Philip II of Macedon defeated Athens and <b>Thebes</b> at Chaeronea in 338 BCE, the poleis lived on as self-governing cities under Hellenistic kings and then under Rome, which had itself grown from a city-state into an empire.</blockquote><a href="/government/city-state">City-state · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>…the word meant simply a monarch who had come to power without hereditary or constitutional right, and poets could use it of kings: the hero of Sophocles’ Oedipus Tyrannus is called tyrannos because he came to <b>Thebes</b> from outside and won its throne, not because he was cruel.</blockquote><a href="/government/tyranny">Tyranny · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Alexander had his leadership confirmed in 336 BCE and punished <b>Thebes</b>' revolt in 335 BCE in the league's name.</blockquote><a href="/state/league-of-corinth">League of Corinth</a></li></ul></section>
